    {
      "id": "shiller-fred",
      "label": "Shiller (Yale) + FRED",
      "sample": false,
      "source": "Stocks and long-term rate: Robert J. Shiller, Yale University (ie_data.xls) \u2014 S&P Composite monthly-average price/dividend, real total-return index, and long-term interest rate (GS10, back-extended pre-1953 by Shiller). Bonds: Shiller's own precomputed monthly total bond return series. Cash: FRED 3-Month Treasury Bill secondary-market rate (TB3MS), 1934 onward; 1928-1933 (pre-TB3MS) uses the Damodaran series' cash figures for those years. Inflation: Shiller's own CPI-U column, annual-average basis.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://shillerdata.com/",
        "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/TB3MS",
        "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/GS10"
      ],
      "retrievedDate": "2026-07-19",
      "methodology": "Nominal calendar-year figures, 1928-2025 (matches the Damodaran series' range). stocks = Shiller's Real Total Return Price index (dividend-reinvested, December-over-December), re-nominalized using Shiller's own CPI change for the year. Shiller's monthly price is a MONTHLY AVERAGE of daily closes, not a month-end close \u2014 a well-known, standard characteristic of this dataset (the same series used across most long-run academic equity-return studies) \u2014 so annual returns derived from it are smoothed relative to official point-to-point index returns; e.g. 2023 comes out ~22.6% here vs. ~26.1% in the Damodaran series' officially-reported figure, both real, both correct for what they measure. bonds = Shiller's own precomputed monthly total-bond-return series (from his GS10 long-rate history, back-extended by Shiller pre-1953), compounded across each calendar year's 12 months \u2014 not a return this app re-derived from the raw yield, avoiding that specific miscalculation risk. cash = FRED TB3MS (3-month T-bill secondary-market rate) annual average, 1934 onward; 1928-1933 predates FRED's TB3MS series and the formal T-bill market (auctions began Dec 1929), so those 6 years reuse the Damodaran series' cash figures. inflation = Shiller's own CPI-U column, annual-average-over-annual-average. Cross-validated against the Damodaran series over the full overlapping range: correlation 0.99 (stocks), 0.96 (bonds), 1.00 (cash), 1.00 (inflation); cumulative $100 growth over 98 years lands in the same order of magnitude on every leg ($1.32M vs $1.16M stocks, $9,579 vs $7,714 bonds, $2,458 vs $2,579 cash); known crash years (1931, 2008 stocks; 1994, 2022 bonds) show correctly-signed, similar-magnitude losses in both series. Remaining year-by-year differences reflect genuine, documented methodology differences (monthly-average vs. point-to-point pricing chief among them), not a data error.",
